{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SHfvUkN9yiNXKNvkH8McpD","type":"tool_result","content":"So that implementation moved to `test/fs-shim.js`. Seed it at\n`build/renderer/lib/platform/fs` and any module that reads or writes\nthrough the shim can be exercised for real. It honours the same\ncontract the shim documents -- promises, a plain `Uint8Array` out of\n`readFile`, a plain object out of `stat`, `exists` swallowing every\nstat failure -- and `test/unit/platform-fs.js` is what holds the real\nshim to that contract, so the two cannot drift silently. It lives in\n`test/` rather than `test/unit/` because `test-unit` globs\n`test/unit/*.js` as test files.\n\n### Stubbing `src/config` in a unit test\n\nA module that reads its paths from `config` can be pointed at a temp\ndirectory by seeding `require.cache[require.resolve('../../build/config')]`\nbefore the first require of the module under test. The stub's exports\nhave to look like a Babel-compiled ES module -- `{ __esModule: true,\ndefault: {...} }` -- because every renderer module imports config as a\ndefault import. `test/unit/migrations.js` uses this to run the real\nmigrations, against the real filesystem, in a throwaway profile.\n\nOnly `src/renderer/lib/**`, `src/shared/**` and `src/engine/**` are\nreachable this way. `build:node` deliberately ignores\n`src/renderer/main.js`, `components/`, `controllers/` and `pages/`\n(Vite owns those), so those modules cannot be unit tested through\n`build/`. See `src/AGENTS.md`.\n\n## Static boundary tests\n\nBecause `components/`, `controllers/` and `main.js` are unreachable\nthrough `build/`, the only automated check on their imports is a\nstatic one. `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` walks the **sources**\nunder `src/renderer/`, skipping `lib/platform/`, and fails on any\ndirect use of an Electron API that has already been migrated behind\nthe shim (`shell` and `clipboard`; plus, since US-027, the\n`@electron/remote` dialog API and the `getCurrentWindow().isVisible` /\n`.isMaximized` reads; plus, since US-028, any import of `fs`; plus,\nsince US-029, `new remote.Menu` / `new remote.MenuItem`; plus, since\nUS-030..US-032, any use of `ipcRenderer` at all and any `wt-` channel\nstring; plus, since US-034, any `require()` of a banned host module).\nEach of US-027..US-032 added its concern to that list as it landed.\nReading source rather than build output is the point -- do not\nrepoint it at `build/`.\n\n**US-034 did not supersede this file.** The lint rule added there\n(`eslint-boundary.config.js`, run by `npm run lint:boundary` and\ncovered by `test/unit/lint-boundary.js`) bans the import\n**specifiers** -- `electron`, `@electron/remote`, `fs`, `os`,\n`crypto`, `http` -- under `src/renderer/`, with\n`src/renderer/lib/platform/` exempt by directory glob and no per-file\nexemption at all. (US-046 dropped the `webtorrent.js` override with\nthe file and US-079 dropped `lib/cast.js`'s the same way, closing\nADR-008; US-046 also added two per-file rules\nthat *tighten* rather than exempt: `lib/platform/ipc-engine.js` is\nbanned from Electron again, and `src/engine/**` -- the sidecar -- is\nbanned from Electron and from every DOM global while keeping Node\nbuiltins. `npm run lint:boundary` lints both trees; a test asserts the\nscript still names both.) It catches a brand new file reaching for a\nhost module,\nwhich the concern-based checks here would miss. It cannot see\n`ipcRenderer.` member reads, `wt-` channel strings, `new remote.Menu`,\nor any `require()` call, which is what this file is for. Add a new\nbanned **specifier** to the lint config; add a new banned **usage** to\nthis file.\n\n`lint-boundary.js` drives the config through ESLint's Node API\n(`new ESLint({ useEslintrc: false, overrideConfigFile })`) rather than\nshelling out, so it can `lintText()` synthetic sources at a chosen\n`filePath` and prove an exemption actually exempts without creating a\nthrowaway file under `src/`. Do not rewrite it to write probe files:\na crashed run would leave them behind for `standard` to trip over.\n\nThe OS-IPC check walked a hand-maintained file list while the\nmigration was partial. Since US-031 it walks the whole renderer, and\nsince US-032 -- with both IPC concerns migrated -- the OS and `wt-*`\nchecks collapsed into one: no `ipcRenderer` and no `wt-` string\nliteral anywhere outside `lib/platform/`, plus an exact assertion on\nwhich renderer files still import a host module at all (since US-039,\nnone). There is no \"left alone for a later story\" counterpart any\nmore, because there is nothing left. The Electron spellings it scans\nfor outlived the host: US-072 deleted every one of them, and the scan\nis what keeps a reflex from bringing one back.\n\nThe `fs` check is the one exception to the rule below: `fs` is a Node\nbuiltin with a single concern behind it, so it bans the specifier\noutright, with no exemption since US-045 deleted `webtorrent.js`.\n\nSince US-046 this file also scans `src/engine/` for the mirror-image\nmistake -- an Electron import or a DOM global in the sidecar -- and\nscans `lib/platform/ipc-engine.js`, which every renderer walk here\nskips because it is inside the shim.\n\nThe checks name **concerns, not module specifiers**. Add a pattern for\nwhat your story actually migrated, not for the import it happens to sit\nnext to. (Up to US-029 that mattered because `remote.Menu` and\n`remote.dialog` lived in the same file and left in different stories.)\n\n## Unit testing a controller\n\nControllers are outside `build:node`, so there is no prebuilt copy to\nrequire. `test/unit/torrent-context-menu.js` compiles the one file\nunder test with `@babel/core` (`transformFileSync(src, { root, cwd })`\npicks up the `babel` key in `package.json`) and writes the result into\n`build/renderer/controllers/`. Emitting it *there* is what makes the\ncontroller's own `../lib/...` imports resolve against the real built\n`build/renderer/lib/**`; a temp directory would not resolve at all.\nStub the shim modules it imports by `require.cache`-seeding their\nbuilt paths, and record dispatches through the real\n`lib/dispatcher`'s `setDispatch()` rather than stubbing it.\n\n`test/unit/os-ipc-call-sites.js` follows the same recipe for the\nUS-030 controllers and `test/unit/engine-ipc-call-sites.js` for the\nUS-032 ones. Cross-file hazards these had to solve, all because\n`tape test/unit/*.js` is a **single process** that requires every file\nbefore running any test:\n\n- **`setDispatch()` is process-wide.** Registering it once at require\n  time means the last file loaded wins for the entire run. Call\n  `setDispatch()` inside each file's per-test `reset()` instead.\n- **A `require.cache` stub of a *built shim module* leaks into later\n  files.** `platform-ipc-os.js` tests the real `ipc-os`, so a stub left\n  at its path breaks it. Withdraw the stub (restore the previous cache\n  entry, or `delete` it) right after requiring the module under test --\n  Babel binds the imported module object at that first require, so the\n  module under test keeps the stub afterwards. Stubs of a package that\n  is not itself a shim module -- `@tauri-apps/api/core`, say -- do not\n  need this; every file that cares seeds its own.\n- **Two files compiling the same controller collide.** The compiled\n  copy is keyed in `require.cache` by its emitted path, so whichever\n  file loads first hands its stubs to the other.\n  `engine-ipc-call-sites.js` emits under a `.engine-test.js` suffix\n  into the same directory, and `media-controller-playability.js` under\n  `.playability-test.js` (the directory is what resolves `../lib/...`,\n  not the file name). Both collide with `os-ipc-call-sites.js`, which\n  emits `media-controller.js` itself. The failure is silent and lands\n  in the *other* file: the loser's recorders stay empty while its own\n  assertions about the modal still pass, so run a new controller test\n  alongside the existing ones, both orders, before believing it.\n  The same collision used to bite two files that both required the\n  main process's `ipc` module; both those files went with the host in\n  US-072. The recipe they used is still the one to reach for when two\n  files need different stubs of the same built module:\n  `delete require.cache[...]` before requiring, then put the previous\n  entry back, so both instances keep the stubs they were built with.\n- **Stub every built module the controller graph reaches, not just the\n  one under test.** Letting a real shim module load here binds *this*\n  file's host stub into it for the whole run, which breaks\n  `platform-clipboard.js` / `platform-ipc-engine.js` later in the\n  glob. `playback-controller` reaches `lib/state` -> `lib/migrations`\n  that way, which is exactly what `migrations.js` tests against.\n- **`migrations.js` never withdraws its `build/config` stub.** It is\n  seeded at require time and left there for the rest of the run, so a\n  later file wanting its own config has to save the previous cache\n  entry, seed its own, `require()` the module under test, and put the\n  previous one back -- `state-file.js`'s `withConfig()` helper is the\n  shape. Pointing that stub's `CONFIG_PATH`, `POSTER_PATH` and\n  `TORRENT_PATH` at a `mkdtemp` directory is what makes it safe to run\n  the *real* `lib/platform/fs` against a throwaway profile.\n- **A recording wrapper beats a fake when the claim is \"it goes\n  through the shim\".** Require the real built shim first, wrap each\n  export in a function that pushes its arguments onto an array and\n  delegates, seed *that* at the shim's path, require the module under\n  test, then restore. The assertions are then about real filesystem\n  behaviour and about the routing at once, and anything reaching for\n  `fs` directly leaves no trace in the array.\n\n## Testing `src/renderer/main.js`\n\n`main.js` cannot be required in a unit test at all -- not even with\nthe compile-it-yourself recipe above. Its module body calls\n`State.load()`, and it pulls in React, ReactDOM, every page and the\nwhole controller graph. `test/unit/main-ipc-call-sites.js` therefore\nchecks it statically, but goes one step past a channel-string ban: it\nextracts every `ipcOs.x` / `ipcEngine.x` member the file reaches for\nand resolves each against the `export function` names in the shim\nsource, so a typo'd or invented export fails there instead of\nsilently becoming `undefined` at runtime. It also asserts the set of\nshim exports covers every channel `main.js` spoke before the\nmigration, which is the only automated guard against a channel being\ndropped.\n\nconst test = require('tape')\nconst fs = require('fs')\nconst path = require('path')\nconst babel = require('@babel/core')\n\n// US-029: the torrent row context menu, built as descriptors and handed\n// to `lib/platform/menu.js`.\n//\n// Controllers are excluded from `build:main` (see the `--ignore` list in\n// package.json), so unlike every other unit test here there is no\n// prebuilt copy to require. We run the same Babel config over the one\n// controller under test and emit it into `build/renderer/controllers/`,\n// where its relative imports resolve against the real built\n// `build/renderer/lib/**`. See test/AGENTS.md.\n\nconst ROOT = path.join(__dirname, '..', '..')\nconst SRC = path.join(\n  ROOT, 'src', 'renderer', 'controllers', 'torrent-list-controller.js'\n)\nconst OUT_DIR = path.join(ROOT, 'build', 'renderer', 'controllers')\nconst OUT = path.join(OUT_DIR, 'torrent-list-controller.js')\n\nfs.mkdirSync(OUT_DIR, { recursive: true })\nfs.writeFileSync(\n  OUT, babel.transformFileSync(SRC, { root: ROOT, cwd: ROOT }).code\n)\n\n// Recording state. Every stub below is a stable object whose behaviour\n// reads from here, because Babel binds each stub's `exports` object once\n// (test/AGENTS.md).\nlet record = null\n\nfunction reset (platform = 'darwin') {\n  record = {\n    menus: [], dispatches: [], copied: [], shell: [], platform\n  }\n  // `lib/dispatcher` holds one process-wide dispatch and every unit\n  // test file that requires it registers its own. Re-register per test\n  // rather than once at require time, or whichever file loads last\n  // wins for the whole run.\n  setDispatch((...args) => record.dispatches.push(args))\n}\n\nfunction stub (modulePath, exports) {\n  const id = require.resolve(modulePath)\n  require.cache[id] = { id, filename: id, loaded: true, exports }\n}\n\nconst BUILT = path.join(ROOT, 'build', 'renderer')\nconst { setDispatch } = require(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'dispatcher'))\n\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'platform', 'menu'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  async showContextMenu (items) { record.menus.push(items) }\n})\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'platform', 'clipboard'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  async writeText (text) { record.copied.push(text) }\n})\n// US-030 moved the reveal and trash channels onto the shell shim.\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'platform', 'shell'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  async showItemInFolder (p) { record.shell.push(['showItemInFolder', p]) },\n  async moveItemToTrash (p) { record.shell.push(['moveItemToTrash', p]) }\n})\n\n// US-072: the reveal item's label is the one string in this menu that\n// depends on the OS, and the controller reads it from `lib/os.js` now\n// rather than from `process.platform` -- the webview it runs in has no\n// such global. It is stubbed rather than left to answer for itself so\n// that both labels are exercised on every machine: read live it would\n// report whichever OS the suite happens to run on, and under plain\n// Node it reports the fallback, so the macOS branch would go\n// unasserted everywhere.\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'os'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  platform: () => record.platform,\n  isDarwin: () => record.platform === 'darwin',\n  isWindows: () => record.platform === 'win32',\n  isLinux: () => record.platform === 'linux'\n})\n\nconst TorrentListController = require(OUT).default\n\nconst INFO_HASH = 'aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa'\n\n// A torrent summary with files, a saved .torrent file and a magnet URI:\n// the longest of the two menu shapes, with every item enabled.\nfunction makeState (overrides = {}) {\n  const summary = Object.assign({\n    torrentKey: 7,\n    infoHash: INFO_HASH,\n    name: 'Test Torrent',\n    magnetURI: 'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:' + INFO_HASH,\n    torrentFileName: 'test.torrent',\n    path: '/tmp/downloads',\n    files: [{ path: 'Test Torrent/one.mp4', length: 10 }]\n  }, overrides.summary || {})\n\n  return {\n    saved: {\n      torrents: [summary],\n      prefs: {\n        downloadPath: '/tmp/downloads',\n        sortByName: overrides.sortByName === true\n      }\n    },\n    location: { url: () => 'home' }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction openMenu (overrides = {}) {\n  reset(overrides.platform)\n  const controller = new TorrentListController(makeState(overrides))\n  controller.openTorrentContextMenu(INFO_HASH)\n  return record.menus[0]\n}\n\nfunction itemNamed (items, label) {\n  return items.find((item) => item.label === label)\n}\n\ntest('the context menu is handed to the menu shim as descriptors',\n  function (t) {\n    const items = openMenu()\n    t.equal(record.menus.length, 1, 'showContextMenu called exactly once')\n    t.ok(Array.isArray(items), 'it is passed an array')\n    for (const item of items) {\n      t.equal(Object.getPrototypeOf(item), Object.prototype,\n        'every entry is a plain object, not a MenuItem')\n    }\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('a torrent with files gets the full menu, in order', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu()\n  t.deepEqual(items.map((i) => i.label || '--separator--'), [\n    'Remove From List',\n    'Remove Data File',\n    '--separator--',\n    'Show in Finder',\n    '--separator--',\n    'Copy Magnet Link to Clipboard',\n    'Copy Instant.io Link to Clipboard',\n    'Save Torrent File As...',\n    '--separator--',\n    'Sort by Name'\n  ], 'labels and separators are unchanged')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// US-072. The label is the only thing that moves, and only the\n// reveal item moves it -- asserted item by item rather than as a\n// whole-menu deepEqual so a regression says which one changed.\ntest('the reveal item is named for the platform file manager',\n  function (t) {\n    for (const [platform, label] of [\n      ['darwin', 'Show in Finder'],\n      ['win32', 'Show in Folder'],\n      ['linux', 'Show in Folder']\n    ]) {\n      const labels = openMenu({ platform }).map((i) => i.label)\n      t.ok(labels.includes(label), `${platform} says ${label}`)\n      t.equal(labels.filter(Boolean).length, 7,\n        `${platform} gets the same seven items`)\n    }\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('a torrent with no files drops the reveal item and its separator',\n  function (t) {\n    const items = openMenu({ summary: { files: undefined } })\n    t.deepEqual(items.map((i) => i.label || '--separator--'), [\n      'Remove From List',\n      'Remove Data File',\n      '--separator--',\n      'Copy Magnet Link to Clipboard',\n      'Copy Instant.io Link to Clipboard',\n      'Save Torrent File As...',\n      '--separator--',\n      'Sort by Name'\n    ], 'only the reveal item and the separator after it are gone')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('separators carry nothing but their type', function (t) {\n  const separators = openMenu().filter((i) => i.type === 'separator')\n  t.equal(separators.length, 3, 'three separators')\n  for (const sep of separators) {\n    t.equal(sep.label, undefined, 'no label')\n    t.equal(sep.click, undefined, 'no click')\n  }\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('Save Torrent File As... is enabled only with a saved .torrent',\n  function (t) {\n    const withFile = itemNamed(openMenu(), 'Save Torrent File As...')\n    t.equal(withFile.enabled, true, 'enabled when torrentFileName is set')\n\n    const without = itemNamed(\n      openMenu({ summary: { torrentFileName: undefined } }),\n      'Save Torrent File As...'\n    )\n    t.equal(without.enabled, false, 'disabled when it is missing')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('no other item sets enabled', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu().filter(\n    (i) => i.type !== 'separator' && i.label !== 'Save Torrent File As...'\n  )\n  t.equal(items.length, 6, 'six unconditionally enabled items')\n  for (const item of items) {\n    t.equal(item.enabled, undefined, item.label + ' leaves enabled alone')\n  }\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('Sort by Name shows a check mark when sorting by name is on',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(itemNamed(openMenu({ sortByName: false }), 'Sort by Name'),\n      'unchecked when off')\n    const items = openMenu({ sortByName: true })\n    t.ok(itemNamed(items, '✓ Sort by Name'),\n      'checked when on')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the remove items dispatch confirmDeleteTorrent', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu()\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Remove From List').click()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Remove Data File').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [\n    ['confirmDeleteTorrent', INFO_HASH, false],\n    ['confirmDeleteTorrent', INFO_HASH, true]\n  ], 'the data flag is the only difference')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('the reveal item asks the host to show the file', function (t) {\n  // `openMenu()` builds on darwin, so this is the Finder spelling. The\n  // click handler behind either label is the same one.\n  const items = openMenu()\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Show in Finder').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.shell, [\n    ['showItemInFolder', path.join('/tmp/downloads', 'Test Torrent')]\n  ], 'sends the torrent folder')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('the copy items write the right text to the clipboard', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu()\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Copy Magnet Link to Clipboard').click()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Copy Instant.io Link to Clipboard').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.copied, [\n    'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:' + INFO_HASH,\n    'https://instant.io/#' + INFO_HASH\n  ], 'magnet URI and instant.io link')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('Save Torrent File As... dispatches with the torrent key',\n  function (t) {\n    const items = openMenu()\n    reset()\n    itemNamed(items, 'Save Torrent File As...').click()\n    t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [['saveTorrentFileAs', 7]],\n      'passes the torrent key, not the info hash')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('Sort by Name toggles the preference', function (t) {\n  const off = openMenu({ sortByName: false })\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(off, 'Sort by Name').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [['updatePreferences', 'sortByName', true]],\n    'turns sorting on')\n\n  const on = openMenu({ sortByName: true })\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(on, '✓ Sort by Name').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [['updatePreferences', 'sortByName', false]],\n    'turns sorting off')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('a click handler takes no arguments from the menu', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu().filter((i) => i.type !== 'separator')\n  for (const item of items) {\n    t.equal(item.click.length, 0, item.label + ' click takes no parameters')\n  }\n  t.end()\n})","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"55616538-4c31-46ba-9fbd-f70a6c9818fa","uuid":"0bb87cba-24f6-4ede-9437-fc1e1b074ff0","timestamp":"2026-10-18T01:09:55.594Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"So that implementation moved to `test/fs-shim.js`. Seed it at\n`build/renderer/lib/platform/fs` and any module that reads or writes\nthrough the shim can be exercised for real. It honours the same\ncontract the shim documents -- promises, a plain `Uint8Array` out of\n`readFile`, a plain object out of `stat`, `exists` swallowing every\nstat failure -- and `test/unit/platform-fs.js` is what holds the real\nshim to that contract, so the two cannot drift silently. It lives in\n`test/` rather than `test/unit/` because `test-unit` globs\n`test/unit/*.js` as test files.\n\n### Stubbing `src/config` in a unit test\n\nA module that reads its paths from `config` can be pointed at a temp\ndirectory by seeding `require.cache[require.resolve('../../build/config')]`\nbefore the first require of the module under test. The stub's exports\nhave to look like a Babel-compiled ES module -- `{ __esModule: true,\ndefault: {...} }` -- because every renderer module imports config as a\ndefault import. `test/unit/migrations.js` uses this to run the real\nmigrations, against the real filesystem, in a throwaway profile.\n\nOnly `src/renderer/lib/**`, `src/shared/**` and `src/engine/**` are\nreachable this way. `build:node` deliberately ignores\n`src/renderer/main.js`, `components/`, `controllers/` and `pages/`\n(Vite owns those), so those modules cannot be unit tested through\n`build/`. See `src/AGENTS.md`.\n\n## Static boundary tests\n\nBecause `components/`, `controllers/` and `main.js` are unreachable\nthrough `build/`, the only automated check on their imports is a\nstatic one. `test/unit/platform-boundary.js` walks the **sources**\nunder `src/renderer/`, skipping `lib/platform/`, and fails on any\ndirect use of an Electron API that has already been migrated behind\nthe shim (`shell` and `clipboard`; plus, since US-027, the\n`@electron/remote` dialog API and the `getCurrentWindow().isVisible` /\n`.isMaximized` reads; plus, since US-028, any import of `fs`; plus,\nsince US-029, `new remote.Menu` / `new remote.MenuItem`; plus, since\nUS-030..US-032, any use of `ipcRenderer` at all and any `wt-` channel\nstring; plus, since US-034, any `require()` of a banned host module).\nEach of US-027..US-032 added its concern to that list as it landed.\nReading source rather than build output is the point -- do not\nrepoint it at `build/`.\n\n**US-034 did not supersede this file.** The lint rule added there\n(`eslint-boundary.config.js`, run by `npm run lint:boundary` and\ncovered by `test/unit/lint-boundary.js`) bans the import\n**specifiers** -- `electron`, `@electron/remote`, `fs`, `os`,\n`crypto`, `http` -- under `src/renderer/`, with\n`src/renderer/lib/platform/` exempt by directory glob and no per-file\nexemption at all. (US-046 dropped the `webtorrent.js` override with\nthe file and US-079 dropped `lib/cast.js`'s the same way, closing\nADR-008; US-046 also added two per-file rules\nthat *tighten* rather than exempt: `lib/platform/ipc-engine.js` is\nbanned from Electron again, and `src/engine/**` -- the sidecar -- is\nbanned from Electron and from every DOM global while keeping Node\nbuiltins. `npm run lint:boundary` lints both trees; a test asserts the\nscript still names both.) It catches a brand new file reaching for a\nhost module,\nwhich the concern-based checks here would miss. It cannot see\n`ipcRenderer.` member reads, `wt-` channel strings, `new remote.Menu`,\nor any `require()` call, which is what this file is for. Add a new\nbanned **specifier** to the lint config; add a new banned **usage** to\nthis file.\n\n`lint-boundary.js` drives the config through ESLint's Node API\n(`new ESLint({ useEslintrc: false, overrideConfigFile })`) rather than\nshelling out, so it can `lintText()` synthetic sources at a chosen\n`filePath` and prove an exemption actually exempts without creating a\nthrowaway file under `src/`. Do not rewrite it to write probe files:\na crashed run would leave them behind for `standard` to trip over.\n\nThe OS-IPC check walked a hand-maintained file list while the\nmigration was partial. Since US-031 it walks the whole renderer, and\nsince US-032 -- with both IPC concerns migrated -- the OS and `wt-*`\nchecks collapsed into one: no `ipcRenderer` and no `wt-` string\nliteral anywhere outside `lib/platform/`, plus an exact assertion on\nwhich renderer files still import a host module at all (since US-039,\nnone). There is no \"left alone for a later story\" counterpart any\nmore, because there is nothing left. The Electron spellings it scans\nfor outlived the host: US-072 deleted every one of them, and the scan\nis what keeps a reflex from bringing one back.\n\nThe `fs` check is the one exception to the rule below: `fs` is a Node\nbuiltin with a single concern behind it, so it bans the specifier\noutright, with no exemption since US-045 deleted `webtorrent.js`.\n\nSince US-046 this file also scans `src/engine/` for the mirror-image\nmistake -- an Electron import or a DOM global in the sidecar -- and\nscans `lib/platform/ipc-engine.js`, which every renderer walk here\nskips because it is inside the shim.\n\nThe checks name **concerns, not module specifiers**. Add a pattern for\nwhat your story actually migrated, not for the import it happens to sit\nnext to. (Up to US-029 that mattered because `remote.Menu` and\n`remote.dialog` lived in the same file and left in different stories.)\n\n## Unit testing a controller\n\nControllers are outside `build:node`, so there is no prebuilt copy to\nrequire. `test/unit/torrent-context-menu.js` compiles the one file\nunder test with `@babel/core` (`transformFileSync(src, { root, cwd })`\npicks up the `babel` key in `package.json`) and writes the result into\n`build/renderer/controllers/`. Emitting it *there* is what makes the\ncontroller's own `../lib/...` imports resolve against the real built\n`build/renderer/lib/**`; a temp directory would not resolve at all.\nStub the shim modules it imports by `require.cache`-seeding their\nbuilt paths, and record dispatches through the real\n`lib/dispatcher`'s `setDispatch()` rather than stubbing it.\n\n`test/unit/os-ipc-call-sites.js` follows the same recipe for the\nUS-030 controllers and `test/unit/engine-ipc-call-sites.js` for the\nUS-032 ones. Cross-file hazards these had to solve, all because\n`tape test/unit/*.js` is a **single process** that requires every file\nbefore running any test:\n\n- **`setDispatch()` is process-wide.** Registering it once at require\n  time means the last file loaded wins for the entire run. Call\n  `setDispatch()` inside each file's per-test `reset()` instead.\n- **A `require.cache` stub of a *built shim module* leaks into later\n  files.** `platform-ipc-os.js` tests the real `ipc-os`, so a stub left\n  at its path breaks it. Withdraw the stub (restore the previous cache\n  entry, or `delete` it) right after requiring the module under test --\n  Babel binds the imported module object at that first require, so the\n  module under test keeps the stub afterwards. Stubs of a package that\n  is not itself a shim module -- `@tauri-apps/api/core`, say -- do not\n  need this; every file that cares seeds its own.\n- **Two files compiling the same controller collide.** The compiled\n  copy is keyed in `require.cache` by its emitted path, so whichever\n  file loads first hands its stubs to the other.\n  `engine-ipc-call-sites.js` emits under a `.engine-test.js` suffix\n  into the same directory, and `media-controller-playability.js` under\n  `.playability-test.js` (the directory is what resolves `../lib/...`,\n  not the file name). Both collide with `os-ipc-call-sites.js`, which\n  emits `media-controller.js` itself. The failure is silent and lands\n  in the *other* file: the loser's recorders stay empty while its own\n  assertions about the modal still pass, so run a new controller test\n  alongside the existing ones, both orders, before believing it.\n  The same collision used to bite two files that both required the\n  main process's `ipc` module; both those files went with the host in\n  US-072. The recipe they used is still the one to reach for when two\n  files need different stubs of the same built module:\n  `delete require.cache[...]` before requiring, then put the previous\n  entry back, so both instances keep the stubs they were built with.\n- **Stub every built module the controller graph reaches, not just the\n  one under test.** Letting a real shim module load here binds *this*\n  file's host stub into it for the whole run, which breaks\n  `platform-clipboard.js` / `platform-ipc-engine.js` later in the\n  glob. `playback-controller` reaches `lib/state` -> `lib/migrations`\n  that way, which is exactly what `migrations.js` tests against.\n- **`migrations.js` never withdraws its `build/config` stub.** It is\n  seeded at require time and left there for the rest of the run, so a\n  later file wanting its own config has to save the previous cache\n  entry, seed its own, `require()` the module under test, and put the\n  previous one back -- `state-file.js`'s `withConfig()` helper is the\n  shape. Pointing that stub's `CONFIG_PATH`, `POSTER_PATH` and\n  `TORRENT_PATH` at a `mkdtemp` directory is what makes it safe to run\n  the *real* `lib/platform/fs` against a throwaway profile.\n- **A recording wrapper beats a fake when the claim is \"it goes\n  through the shim\".** Require the real built shim first, wrap each\n  export in a function that pushes its arguments onto an array and\n  delegates, seed *that* at the shim's path, require the module under\n  test, then restore. The assertions are then about real filesystem\n  behaviour and about the routing at once, and anything reaching for\n  `fs` directly leaves no trace in the array.\n\n## Testing `src/renderer/main.js`\n\n`main.js` cannot be required in a unit test at all -- not even with\nthe compile-it-yourself recipe above. Its module body calls\n`State.load()`, and it pulls in React, ReactDOM, every page and the\nwhole controller graph. `test/unit/main-ipc-call-sites.js` therefore\nchecks it statically, but goes one step past a channel-string ban: it\nextracts every `ipcOs.x` / `ipcEngine.x` member the file reaches for\nand resolves each against the `export function` names in the shim\nsource, so a typo'd or invented export fails there instead of\nsilently becoming `undefined` at runtime. It also asserts the set of\nshim exports covers every channel `main.js` spoke before the\nmigration, which is the only automated guard against a channel being\ndropped.\n\nconst test = require('tape')\nconst fs = require('fs')\nconst path = require('path')\nconst babel = require('@babel/core')\n\n// US-029: the torrent row context menu, built as descriptors and handed\n// to `lib/platform/menu.js`.\n//\n// Controllers are excluded from `build:main` (see the `--ignore` list in\n// package.json), so unlike every other unit test here there is no\n// prebuilt copy to require. We run the same Babel config over the one\n// controller under test and emit it into `build/renderer/controllers/`,\n// where its relative imports resolve against the real built\n// `build/renderer/lib/**`. See test/AGENTS.md.\n\nconst ROOT = path.join(__dirname, '..', '..')\nconst SRC = path.join(\n  ROOT, 'src', 'renderer', 'controllers', 'torrent-list-controller.js'\n)\nconst OUT_DIR = path.join(ROOT, 'build', 'renderer', 'controllers')\nconst OUT = path.join(OUT_DIR, 'torrent-list-controller.js')\n\nfs.mkdirSync(OUT_DIR, { recursive: true })\nfs.writeFileSync(\n  OUT, babel.transformFileSync(SRC, { root: ROOT, cwd: ROOT }).code\n)\n\n// Recording state. Every stub below is a stable object whose behaviour\n// reads from here, because Babel binds each stub's `exports` object once\n// (test/AGENTS.md).\nlet record = null\n\nfunction reset (platform = 'darwin') {\n  record = {\n    menus: [], dispatches: [], copied: [], shell: [], platform\n  }\n  // `lib/dispatcher` holds one process-wide dispatch and every unit\n  // test file that requires it registers its own. Re-register per test\n  // rather than once at require time, or whichever file loads last\n  // wins for the whole run.\n  setDispatch((...args) => record.dispatches.push(args))\n}\n\nfunction stub (modulePath, exports) {\n  const id = require.resolve(modulePath)\n  require.cache[id] = { id, filename: id, loaded: true, exports }\n}\n\nconst BUILT = path.join(ROOT, 'build', 'renderer')\nconst { setDispatch } = require(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'dispatcher'))\n\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'platform', 'menu'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  async showContextMenu (items) { record.menus.push(items) }\n})\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'platform', 'clipboard'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  async writeText (text) { record.copied.push(text) }\n})\n// US-030 moved the reveal and trash channels onto the shell shim.\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'platform', 'shell'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  async showItemInFolder (p) { record.shell.push(['showItemInFolder', p]) },\n  async moveItemToTrash (p) { record.shell.push(['moveItemToTrash', p]) }\n})\n\n// US-072: the reveal item's label is the one string in this menu that\n// depends on the OS, and the controller reads it from `lib/os.js` now\n// rather than from `process.platform` -- the webview it runs in has no\n// such global. It is stubbed rather than left to answer for itself so\n// that both labels are exercised on every machine: read live it would\n// report whichever OS the suite happens to run on, and under plain\n// Node it reports the fallback, so the macOS branch would go\n// unasserted everywhere.\nstub(path.join(BUILT, 'lib', 'os'), {\n  __esModule: true,\n  platform: () => record.platform,\n  isDarwin: () => record.platform === 'darwin',\n  isWindows: () => record.platform === 'win32',\n  isLinux: () => record.platform === 'linux'\n})\n\nconst TorrentListController = require(OUT).default\n\nconst INFO_HASH = 'aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa'\n\n// A torrent summary with files, a saved .torrent file and a magnet URI:\n// the longest of the two menu shapes, with every item enabled.\nfunction makeState (overrides = {}) {\n  const summary = Object.assign({\n    torrentKey: 7,\n    infoHash: INFO_HASH,\n    name: 'Test Torrent',\n    magnetURI: 'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:' + INFO_HASH,\n    torrentFileName: 'test.torrent',\n    path: '/tmp/downloads',\n    files: [{ path: 'Test Torrent/one.mp4', length: 10 }]\n  }, overrides.summary || {})\n\n  return {\n    saved: {\n      torrents: [summary],\n      prefs: {\n        downloadPath: '/tmp/downloads',\n        sortByName: overrides.sortByName === true\n      }\n    },\n    location: { url: () => 'home' }\n  }\n}\n\nfunction openMenu (overrides = {}) {\n  reset(overrides.platform)\n  const controller = new TorrentListController(makeState(overrides))\n  controller.openTorrentContextMenu(INFO_HASH)\n  return record.menus[0]\n}\n\nfunction itemNamed (items, label) {\n  return items.find((item) => item.label === label)\n}\n\ntest('the context menu is handed to the menu shim as descriptors',\n  function (t) {\n    const items = openMenu()\n    t.equal(record.menus.length, 1, 'showContextMenu called exactly once')\n    t.ok(Array.isArray(items), 'it is passed an array')\n    for (const item of items) {\n      t.equal(Object.getPrototypeOf(item), Object.prototype,\n        'every entry is a plain object, not a MenuItem')\n    }\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('a torrent with files gets the full menu, in order', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu()\n  t.deepEqual(items.map((i) => i.label || '--separator--'), [\n    'Remove From List',\n    'Remove Data File',\n    '--separator--',\n    'Show in Finder',\n    '--separator--',\n    'Copy Magnet Link to Clipboard',\n    'Copy Instant.io Link to Clipboard',\n    'Save Torrent File As...',\n    '--separator--',\n    'Sort by Name'\n  ], 'labels and separators are unchanged')\n  t.end()\n})\n\n// US-072. The label is the only thing that moves, and only the\n// reveal item moves it -- asserted item by item rather than as a\n// whole-menu deepEqual so a regression says which one changed.\ntest('the reveal item is named for the platform file manager',\n  function (t) {\n    for (const [platform, label] of [\n      ['darwin', 'Show in Finder'],\n      ['win32', 'Show in Folder'],\n      ['linux', 'Show in Folder']\n    ]) {\n      const labels = openMenu({ platform }).map((i) => i.label)\n      t.ok(labels.includes(label), `${platform} says ${label}`)\n      t.equal(labels.filter(Boolean).length, 7,\n        `${platform} gets the same seven items`)\n    }\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('a torrent with no files drops the reveal item and its separator',\n  function (t) {\n    const items = openMenu({ summary: { files: undefined } })\n    t.deepEqual(items.map((i) => i.label || '--separator--'), [\n      'Remove From List',\n      'Remove Data File',\n      '--separator--',\n      'Copy Magnet Link to Clipboard',\n      'Copy Instant.io Link to Clipboard',\n      'Save Torrent File As...',\n      '--separator--',\n      'Sort by Name'\n    ], 'only the reveal item and the separator after it are gone')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('separators carry nothing but their type', function (t) {\n  const separators = openMenu().filter((i) => i.type === 'separator')\n  t.equal(separators.length, 3, 'three separators')\n  for (const sep of separators) {\n    t.equal(sep.label, undefined, 'no label')\n    t.equal(sep.click, undefined, 'no click')\n  }\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('Save Torrent File As... is enabled only with a saved .torrent',\n  function (t) {\n    const withFile = itemNamed(openMenu(), 'Save Torrent File As...')\n    t.equal(withFile.enabled, true, 'enabled when torrentFileName is set')\n\n    const without = itemNamed(\n      openMenu({ summary: { torrentFileName: undefined } }),\n      'Save Torrent File As...'\n    )\n    t.equal(without.enabled, false, 'disabled when it is missing')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('no other item sets enabled', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu().filter(\n    (i) => i.type !== 'separator' && i.label !== 'Save Torrent File As...'\n  )\n  t.equal(items.length, 6, 'six unconditionally enabled items')\n  for (const item of items) {\n    t.equal(item.enabled, undefined, item.label + ' leaves enabled alone')\n  }\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('Sort by Name shows a check mark when sorting by name is on',\n  function (t) {\n    t.ok(itemNamed(openMenu({ sortByName: false }), 'Sort by Name'),\n      'unchecked when off')\n    const items = openMenu({ sortByName: true })\n    t.ok(itemNamed(items, '✓ Sort by Name'),\n      'checked when on')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('the remove items dispatch confirmDeleteTorrent', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu()\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Remove From List').click()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Remove Data File').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [\n    ['confirmDeleteTorrent', INFO_HASH, false],\n    ['confirmDeleteTorrent', INFO_HASH, true]\n  ], 'the data flag is the only difference')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('the reveal item asks the host to show the file', function (t) {\n  // `openMenu()` builds on darwin, so this is the Finder spelling. The\n  // click handler behind either label is the same one.\n  const items = openMenu()\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Show in Finder').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.shell, [\n    ['showItemInFolder', path.join('/tmp/downloads', 'Test Torrent')]\n  ], 'sends the torrent folder')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('the copy items write the right text to the clipboard', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu()\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Copy Magnet Link to Clipboard').click()\n  itemNamed(items, 'Copy Instant.io Link to Clipboard').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.copied, [\n    'magnet:?xt=urn:btih:' + INFO_HASH,\n    'https://instant.io/#' + INFO_HASH\n  ], 'magnet URI and instant.io link')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('Save Torrent File As... dispatches with the torrent key',\n  function (t) {\n    const items = openMenu()\n    reset()\n    itemNamed(items, 'Save Torrent File As...').click()\n    t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [['saveTorrentFileAs', 7]],\n      'passes the torrent key, not the info hash')\n    t.end()\n  })\n\ntest('Sort by Name toggles the preference', function (t) {\n  const off = openMenu({ sortByName: false })\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(off, 'Sort by Name').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [['updatePreferences', 'sortByName', true]],\n    'turns sorting on')\n\n  const on = openMenu({ sortByName: true })\n  reset()\n  itemNamed(on, '✓ Sort by Name').click()\n  t.deepEqual(record.dispatches, [['updatePreferences', 'sortByName', false]],\n    'turns sorting off')\n  t.end()\n})\n\ntest('a click handler takes no arguments from the menu', function (t) {\n  const items = openMenu().filter((i) => i.type !== 'separator')\n  for (const item of items) {\n    t.equal(item.click.length, 0, item.label + ' click takes no parameters')\n  }\n  t.end()\n})","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SHfvUkN9yiNXKNvkH8McpD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
